#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105557 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82c825b9-fc36-510c-982e-e4596ab2ce24" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Distance to a Circle's Center"}] :question/id #uuid "2b2211b8-cc3f-5dd6-9dc0-1dc1a7f69513" :question/math-academy-id "e-3166" :question/problem "The diagram below shows a circle of radius $4,$ a point $C$ that lies on the circle, and a third point $P$ on the plane.\n\n \n\n![](images/e6/e6ede881702a8713cf9c878f9a9d7fbdd0bdcb14bcab500d3cf256cc9cc84463.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\nI. $OP \\leq 4$\n\nII. $OC = 4$\n\nIII. $OP > 4$" :question/worked-solution "The point $C$ lies on the circle itself. So, the distance from $O$ to $C$ is equal to the radius of the circle:\n\n$$\nOC = 4.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the point $P$ lies strictly outside the circle. So, the distance between $P$ and the center $O$ of the circle must be greater than the radius:\n\n$$\nOP > OC = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa963eb-82a7-56cb-a8b8-f9291adec2d0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of a Line Segment With Endpoints on a Circle and Passing Through the Center"}] :question/id #uuid "9e6ebec8-4c08-5bc9-8a8c-4c6cedb2bd8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3167" :question/problem "Consider the diagram below. What is the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ if $OQ=3?$\n\n![](images/4e/4e32346cc949b0f17ed219e44d4600abd23a0839a3855fcf76ff4060f322698c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the points $A,$ $B,$ and $Q$ lie on the circle. From the diagram, the radius of the circle is $r =OQ=3.$ \n\nTo find $AB,$ we first notice that\n\n$$\nAB = AO + OB.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overline{AO}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because the radius is $3,$ we have\n\n$$\nAO=3, \\qquad OB=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nAB = AO+OB = 3 + 3= 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2401d489-0970-54a8-a70d-9e8613458f54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Line Segments Associated with a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "cb990fba-991a-58c8-a404-54353183903c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3168" :question/problem "![](images/60/606c8bb2352215fe5c34acb78742034df016b8f41c7a6194b700e861f4f35596.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\nI. $\\overline{BC}$ is a chord of the circle\n\nII. $\\overline{BC}$ is a diameter of the circle\n\nIII. $\\overline{OC}$ is a radius of the circle" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As we can see, both endpoints $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle. Therefore,  $\\overline{BC}$ is a chord.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see, both endpoints $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle and $\\overline{BC}$ passes through the center $O.$ Therefore, $\\overline{BC}$ is a diameter.\n\n- Statement III is true. The segment $\\overline{OC}$ connects a point on the circle with its center. So, $\\overline{OC}$ is a radius.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2401d489-0970-54a8-a70d-9e8613458f54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Line Segments Associated with a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45aa58b9-6f9d-5b0b-a8d7-dbbafa1625ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496807} {:answer/id #uuid "53b45703-88b5-5b52-a0db-e1f224d38b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496837} {:answer/id #uuid "8994e3eb-5222-572c-9b5f-cd69f4aeb8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496945} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2a2824-06c3-5f61-8389-de1fff068096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496980} {:answer/id #uuid "9c550129-5360-5bed-af8e-756c1c72c5e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53b45703-88b5-5b52-a0db-e1f224d38b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496837} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1f9ad8-334e-512b-be89-204c750f3ba0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c258cfe8-9152-5d49-a8e8-a1c8668a4029" :question/math-academy-id "q-121593" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cf46e7f0e0405d2c2fa71c5ed90743a80242817bac359636a51b1f6f74273ce.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\nI. $\\overset{―}{OQ}$ is a chord of the circle\n\nII. $\\overset{―}{OR}$ is a radius of the circle\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{QR}$ is a diameter of the circle" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since the endpoint $O$ does not lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{OQ}$ is a radius, but not a chord.\n\n- Statement II is true. The segment $\\overset{―}{OR}$ connects a point on the circle with its center. So, $\\overset{―}{OR}$ is a radius.\n\n- Statement III is false since the segment $\\overset{―}{QR}$ does not pass through the circle's center. Therefore, $\\overset{―}{QR}$ is a chord but not a diameter.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82c825b9-fc36-510c-982e-e4596ab2ce24" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Distance to a Circle's Center"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f567857-fdbe-5e15-8e1d-74e3283869bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "OB=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496761} {:answer/id #uuid "7be8d34a-aebd-5a58-9df9-64a55051729c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "OB=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496917} {:answer/id #uuid "88cb912a-d475-5daf-ae75-cd53c65175da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "OB=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496941} {:answer/id #uuid "9213be05-0d07-5166-8b9a-6d44745871e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "OB=14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496962} {:answer/id #uuid "b4594394-849c-5113-9f80-38b270c23448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "OB=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f567857-fdbe-5e15-8e1d-74e3283869bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "OB=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496761} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f08898-f474-54e8-8d99-f01d18e59d63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6da0fd72-e546-5bf3-b264-a420d5d83b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-21160" :question/problem "Consider a circle with center $O$ and radius $r=14,$ and the point $B$ that lies in the same plane as the circle. Which of the following conditions might be true if it's known that the point $B$ lies outside the circle?" :question/worked-solution "We will sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/60/60056e6bc44989185f3707ba013bb81d440b8a7060e5c754608e8c5977db687e.png)\n\nSince $B$ lies outside the circle, the distance between $B$ and the center $O$ of the circle must be greater than the radius. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\nOB>r=14.\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only $OB=15$ satisfies the above inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2401d489-0970-54a8-a70d-9e8613458f54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Line Segments Associated with a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9b6ece2-c175-5a62-a4c8-d3a7bc1bcb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104681} {:answer/id #uuid "bac5f487-8251-5e3f-a25d-1940879a8ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104682} {:answer/id #uuid "d191d7ec-f08f-53da-9bdf-ef6d460a8ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104683} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1687fe-5277-552d-af56-af2b924dbf53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104684} {:answer/id #uuid "5333d1c5-bab1-5a7b-8948-71d6f629ad61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc1687fe-5277-552d-af56-af2b924dbf53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104684} :answer-field/id #uuid "f61b895f-2b53-547b-9ce6-170508475559" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36da278f-fa98-5fed-9b0f-aa9974a3b0ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-53527" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f772ad4b54217a479b63a6919a915af7bd8f44013687dae29712bf09624c68b.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\nI. $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle\n\nII. $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a radius of the circle\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{OA}$ is a chord of the circle" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As we can see, both endpoints $A$ and $B$ lie on the circle and $\\overset{―}{AB}$ passes through the center $O.$ Therefore, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter.\n\n- Statement II is false since both endpoints of $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a chord but not a radius.\n\n- Statement III is false since the endpoint $O$ does not lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{OA}$ is a radius but not a chord.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa963eb-82a7-56cb-a8b8-f9291adec2d0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of a Line Segment With Endpoints on a Circle and Passing Through the Center"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32354b47-0818-52d1-b430-1f203ac78c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496766} {:answer/id #uuid "33433780-d909-5f95-bb64-c733b33ba820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496769} {:answer/id #uuid "6cba61fa-cff2-5bde-af5a-ac0af7bd9428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496893} {:answer/id #uuid "7791b87a-f95b-5be4-8e28-be1e17f509d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496909} {:answer/id #uuid "db463c29-47fb-55ec-bf65-7ce5fd52451a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cba61fa-cff2-5bde-af5a-ac0af7bd9428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496893} :answer-field/id #uuid "53dccb12-0521-56c2-b79b-899f7857a8fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7240a23b-2aa5-51b9-83e2-173faee26862" :question/math-academy-id "q-74592" :question/problem "![](images/29/2981b6af8c364d33211155585768059d7fdde44eb99717019ab6d668ff09f6b3.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ if $OC=16?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ lie on the circle. From the diagram, the radius of the circle is $r=OC=16.$ \n\nTo find $AB,$ we first notice that\n\n$$\nAB=AO+OB.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overset{―}{AO}$ and $\\overset{―}{OB}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because the radius is $16,$ we have \n\n$$\nAO=16,\\,OB=16.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nAB=AO+OB=16+16=32.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa963eb-82a7-56cb-a8b8-f9291adec2d0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of a Line Segment With Endpoints on a Circle and Passing Through the Center"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef873a9-5bec-5e2b-b660-6cbad6be6522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496687} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb375fa-1d11-5eff-996e-fecfe5633667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496897} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0d43d5-9364-564f-877e-74e4ddf33628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496955} {:answer/id #uuid "a478fe9d-3b97-5496-b0b5-0a57fbd46986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496997} {:answer/id #uuid "d45e7324-0c89-55ac-8a0f-3d2e3da787f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ef873a9-5bec-5e2b-b660-6cbad6be6522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496687} :answer-field/id #uuid "85a6d838-1d50-5cf7-85ea-9d3d39581030" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eefb1780-b1c4-5fe1-ae57-be7c2236e61c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74666" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec14b1461a3125c2b4c320166230980ee33cbd004e07692e2c7763868f0edaa1.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $\\overset{―}{OC}$ if $AB=15?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ lie on the circle. From the diagram, the radius of the circle is $r=OC.$ \n\nWe first notice that\n\n$$\nAB=AO+OB.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overset{―}{AO}$ and $\\overset{―}{OB}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because $AB=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =15 \\\\ AO+OB & =15 \\\\ r+r & =15 \\\\ 2r & =15 \\\\ r & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nOC=r=7.5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82c825b9-fc36-510c-982e-e4596ab2ce24" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Distance to a Circle's Center"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1dce06-b6df-5198-871d-5f7df6305d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496760} {:answer/id #uuid "38ebd1ac-f05f-5684-ba45-1baf51d3c9dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496780} {:answer/id #uuid "7f834f9e-e31b-50c0-9395-61cc663eabdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496921} {:answer/id #uuid "b595c308-c8b7-5ce8-afa4-41e46170e1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497032} {:answer/id #uuid "d0298301-8681-50d7-9497-fba8382db3e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f1dce06-b6df-5198-871d-5f7df6305d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496760} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e02ca1d-a32c-5641-a808-7318b1025077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d4d18d1-b26f-52d1-a7f8-17af1daba334" :question/math-academy-id "q-74672" :question/problem "![](images/44/44e48aadd601b6b9c66358bac848748f6a752d2144df500dfb0dfcf6aec105da.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $17,$ a point $C$ that lies on the circle, and a third point $Q$ on the plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $OQ<17$\n\nII. $OQ>17$\n\nIII. $OC>17$" :question/worked-solution "The point $C$ lies on the circle itself. So, the distance from $O$ to $C$ is equal to the radius of the circle:\n\n$$\nOC=17\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the point $Q$ lies outside the circle. So, the distance between $Q$ and the center $O$ of the circle must be greater than the radius:\n\n$$\nOQ>OC=17\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement II is true, while statements I and III are false."}]}
